00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:12,000
Anyone who wanted to enter the tournament could enter with any strategy they want, and then tit for tat won.

231
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
But here's what's really impressive.

232
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:22,000
The experimenters redid the tournament after reporting all the results, full publicity, and then there were more strategies entered.

233
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:28,000
So I think it was something like 50 or 60 strategies were entered, and tit for tat won the second round.

234
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:34,000
And that's really big because then it's like everyone is thinking, well, tit for tat is the strategy to beat.

235
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:39,000
And so I will find something ideally suited to beat tit for tat.

236
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:44,000
But the problem was that while people were able to come up with strategies to beat tit for tat,

237
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:50,000
they did so much worse against the other strategies, that tit for tat still prevailed in the tournament.

238
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:56,000
My understanding is that Gordon Tullock was in the tournament, and he was the guy who just put in the always betray strategy.

239
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:11,000
So why don't we go and we'll take our 10 minute break now, and then resume.

240
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:22,000
So given that the focus here says that there will normally be an infinity of equilibria, which one will actually happen?

241
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:28,000
Focal points give us a lot of guidance. It's hard to formally model, so again, in order to actually really talk about this,

242
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:34,000
you need to be someone who combines econ, psychology, and sociology, which not that many people want to do.

243
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
So you have to read three whole fields.

244
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:41,000
I can imagine the burden on someone who wants to figure out what's going to happen in the world,

245
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
and have to read three different areas.

246
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:48,000
My view is if you really want to understand the world, you should read in all these areas.

247
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:52,000
So far I don't think any students have ever taken me up on this advice.

248
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:56,000
Of course that means the opposite is going to happen now, with the Gamber's Fallacy.

249
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:01,000
If I fail so far, then my good luck is coming in dice, right?

250
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
So yeah, of course.

251
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,000
Moving on to reputation.

252
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:13,000
It's very common for economists who see firms appearing to lose money on purpose to say,

253
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
ah, well the reason they're doing it is because of reputation.

254
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,000
And you might ask, does that make sense?

255
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,000
Can reputational models be rationalized in the same way that we're doing?

256
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:30,000
So rationalize using the key to play, and the answer, as you might guess, is yes.

257
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:35,000
You can actually rationalize profit-losing behavior as, in the short run,

258
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:40,000
profit-losing behavior as part of a long-run profit-enhancing strategy.

259
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:46,000
So suppose that when a store decides to cheat a customer,

260
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,000
customers decide whether to buy or not.

261
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,000
So we can have a little model like this.

262
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:57,000
So we can have a model where firms either cheat or don't,

263
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:01,000
and then customers either buy or don't.

264
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
We can have a payoff something like this.

265
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:07,000
So if the firm cheats and the customer buys and the firm gets a nice profit,

266
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
and the customer's worse off and they get bought,

267
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:14,000
if the firm doesn't cheat and the customer buys, then they both get some surplus.

268
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:18,000
And if no purchase happens, then no one gets any surplus.

269
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
So you get something like this.

270
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:25,000
And if you then just apply weak dominance, it looks like the store owner will always cheat.

271
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
So under weak dominance, the store owner will always cheat,

272
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:32,000
because if the customer is not going to buy anyway, then he'll just get a buy, he gets zero.

273
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
But if he's going to buy, then when you cheat, you get ten instead of five.

274
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:39,000
So it seems like people will always cheat, right?

275
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:43,000
But suppose the customers can condition their purchasing behavior

276
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:46,000
based upon whether they've seen you cheat in the past.

277
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
And notice, you don't even need to have been the person who has cheated.

278
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:55,000
It can just be that there's a big line of customers, each of whom goes and buys one item.

279
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:01,000
But each person can go and see whether the last person got cheated or not.

280
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,000
And then they decide, well, do I feel like buying from you or not?

281
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:09,000
Or, of course, in a Walmart setting, you could look at the reviews,

282
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and if the reviews are worse than, say, 90 percent, then you don't buy.

283
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:15,000
So something like that.

284
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
So anyway, suppose customers have the option of playing buy,

285
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
and they don't pass cheating, and don't otherwise,

286
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
and then we can see whether we have an equilibrium.

287
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
And since I put zeros in here, the math is easy.

288
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:31,000
So basically, you can either go and get the infinite sum of fives.

289
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,000
You can get the don't cheat, buy, pay off forever.

290
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:39,000
Or you can rip someone off once and then no one ever buys from you again.

291
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
And then you get 10.

292
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
And you go through, and if you work this out,

293
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,000
it looks like the critical value of beta, beta star, is going to be equal to.5.

294
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:52,000
So as long as beta is.5 or higher, cooperation will be possible.

295
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
So this is the model.

296
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:57,000
Now, the applications of reputation are truly endless.

297
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:02,000
Right here, I'm going to raise the status of Professor Dan Klein.

298
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,000
So what is Dan Klein?

299
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,000
A visionary is what he is.

300
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
So he didn't quite invent the Internet.

301
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:14,000
But before Internet commerce was anything noticeable,

302
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:19,000
Dan Klein actually went and edited a whole book called Reputation,

303
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,000
writing a subtitle like Studies in the Voluntary Elicitation of Good Behavior.

304
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
And in the book, he just put together all of the main evidence

305
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
on what reputation can accomplish.

306
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:31,000
And he said that reputation can do a lot of things

307
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:35,000
that many economists at the time would have said it could not do.

308
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:38,000
And in particular, if you go and read it now,

309
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
it seems like he's kind of obvious.

310
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
Like, wasn't he just writing about e-commerce?

311
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:45,000
No, because there basically was no e-commerce at the time.

312
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
He just wrote a book about what could happen,

313
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:50,000
and then something amazing happened.

314
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:54,000
What an economist said could happen, that didn't exist at the time, did occur.

315
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Wow. All right.

316
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
So that's basically the gold standard of prediction, right?

317
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:24,000
To say that something that doesn't yet exist...


